# ============================================================
# Git Ignore for DNSCrypt Smart Filter
# Version: v1.2.0 (Global Edition)
# Author: gasciljh
# Repository: https://github.com/gasciljh/dnscrypt-proxy-webui
# ============================================================
# Purpose:
#   Prevents build artifacts, runtime files, secrets, and IDE
#   files from being accidentally committed.
#
# Scope:
#   Patterns are defined only for file types that actually
#   exist or are created by this repository at runtime.
#
# v1.1.0 notes (kept):
#   • Removed two no-op negations:
#       - `!proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version` in [2] (the patterns
#         above could never match it).
#       - `!proxy/*.key.pub` in [13] (`*.key` cannot match
#         `*.key.pub`).
#   • Added clarifying comments for sections that previously
#     had none.
#
# ============================================================
# v1.2.0 changes:
#   • Version bumped from v1.1.0 to v1.2.0.
#   • 🔧 FIX (this revision): Corrected the IDE negation pattern
#     in §[12]. The previous pattern `.vscode/` ignored the
#     entire directory, which made the following
#     `!.vscode/settings.json.example` a NO-OP. Git cannot
#     un-ignore a file inside an ignored directory.
#
#     The corrected pattern is `.vscode/*`, which ignores only
#     the CONTENTS of `.vscode/` (not the directory itself),
#     allowing the negation to work as intended.
#     This fix is now tracked as HARD-GI-06 (see §[12]).
#
#   • Added a cross-reference from §[1] (`dist/`) to `make
#     package`, which creates the packaged ZIP + checksum.
#
#   • Added a note that the v1.2.0 verification targets
#     (`make check-docs`, `make check-backup`, `make check-all`)
#     do NOT create any files — they are read-only checks.
#
#   • Confirmed: the persistent backup directory on the device
#     — `/sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/` — NEVER appears in the
#     repository. It lives on the Android device's SD card, not
#     in the git working tree. No .gitignore rule is needed for
#     it (and none is provided). This is deliberate: adding a
#     path that can never appear would be noise.
#
#   • Re-audited sections [1]–[19] against the v1.2.0 file
#     inventory. No new build artifact or runtime file was
#     introduced by the data-preservation release.
#
#   • Global English edition: all comments are English-only.
#     No Arabic strings exist in this file.
#
# Reminder:
#   If you add a new pattern here, add a matching exclusion in
#   .gitattributes if the file is binary, or a matching section
#   in .editorconfig if it has specific formatting rules.
#
# Cross-references (v1.2.0):
#   • docs/BACKUP.md      — backup system reference
#   • docs/EMERGENCY.md   — emergency recovery guide
#   • docs/UPGRADE.md §3.1 — v1.1.0 → v1.2.0 upgrade path
#   • Makefile            — `make package`, `make check-docs`,
#                           `make check-backup`, `make check-all`
#   • .github/workflows/codeql.yml — HARD-CQ-08 note about
#                                    `proxy/.codeql-analyze`
#
# ============================================================
# v1.2.0 (Global Edition) — Additional hardening
# ============================================================
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-01 — Removed the redundant `.idea/**/*` entries
#     in §[12]. Since `.idea/` (with trailing slash) ignores the
#     entire directory, the granular entries inside it could
#     never take effect. If a future exception is needed,
#     switch to the `.idea/*` + `!.idea/<file>` pattern.
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-02 — Added `*.sarif` to §[1] (build artifacts).
#     CodeQL produces SARIF files; when the analysis is run
#     locally (rather than on CI), those files would otherwise
#     appear in `git status`. (Moved from §[8] where it was
#     misleadingly grouped under "Go build artifacts".)
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-03 — Added `*.sha256` to §[1]. The packaged
#     checksum is already ignored via `dist/`, but a developer
#     running `sha256sum` manually could produce one elsewhere.
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-04 — Added `*.tmp-*` to §[4]. Some `mktemp`
#     variants produce `<file>.tmp-<random>` (dash instead of
#     underscore). The existing `*.tmp_*` covers the underscore
#     form used by `atomicWriteFile`; the new pattern covers
#     the dash form used by external tooling.
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-05 — Added `/.codeql-analyze` and
#     `proxy/.codeql-analyze` to §[1]. This closes H-02 from
#     the original audit and validates HARD-CQ-08 in
#     `.github/workflows/codeql.yml`, which asserts that the
#     pattern exists here. (H-02 is the same fix as
#     HARD-GI-05; the two identifiers refer to one change.)
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-06 — Corrected the `.vscode` negation pattern
#     in §[12] from `.vscode/` to `.vscode/*`. This is the
#     change previously documented only as "🔧 v1.2.0 FIX";
#     it now has a stable identifier for cross-referencing.
#
#   🛡️ HARD-GI-07 — Removed all `/tmp/...` entries from §[9]
#     and §[19]. In `.gitignore`, a leading `/` anchors the
#     pattern to the repository root, NOT to the filesystem
#     root. Thus `/tmp/dnscrypt-dev/` meant
#     `<repo_root>/tmp/dnscrypt-dev/` — a path that does not
#     exist. Real files under `/tmp/` never enter the working
#     tree, so the patterns were pure noise; worse, a local
#     `tmp/` directory inside the repo would have been
#     silently ignored. Removed in full.
# ============================================================

# ============================================================
# [1] Build artifacts
# ============================================================
# Outputs produced locally or in CI:
#   • build/       — local build tree (if ever used)
#   • proxy/build/ — WebUI binaries output (from build.sh)
#   • dist/        — Magisk ZIP + checksum
#                    (from `make package` → package_module.sh)
#   • *.zip        — any ZIP (defensive)
#   • *.tar.*      — any tarball (defensive)
#   • *.sha256     — checksum sidecar files (defensive, HARD-GI-03)
#   • *.sarif      — CodeQL analysis output (HARD-GI-02)
#   • .codeql-analyze — CodeQL build output (HARD-GI-05, closes H-02)
#
# v1.2.0 note: `make package` (repaired in this release) creates
# `dist/dnscrypt-webui-<VERSION>-module.zip` and its `.sha256`.
# Both live under `dist/`, which is ignored here.
#
# HARD-GI-05 note: The CodeQL workflow
# (.github/workflows/codeql.yml) writes a temporary build
# artifact to `proxy/.codeql-analyze`. It is removed by a trap
# handler on any exit path, but a manual build or a partial
# failure could leave the file behind. The two patterns below
# keep it out of `git status` in either case:
#   • `/.codeql-analyze`     — root-level (if a manual build
#                              is run from the wrong directory)
#   • `proxy/.codeql-analyze` — the canonical location
#
# See HARD-CQ-08 in .github/workflows/codeql.yml for the full
# rationale.
# ============================================================
build/
proxy/build/
dist/
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2
*.tar.xz
*.sha256
*.sarif
/.codeql-analyze
proxy/.codeql-analyze

# ============================================================
# [2] WebUI binaries (built by build.sh / release.yml)
# ============================================================
# These files are never committed — they are built from main.go
# and injected into the Magisk ZIP at packaging time.
#
# Patterns cover:
#   • proxy/dnscrypt-webui-arm64
#   • proxy/dnscrypt-webui-arm
#   • proxy/dnscrypt-webui-amd64
#   • proxy/dnscrypt-webui-386
#   • dnscrypt-webui-* (root-level, from --package)
#
# ⚠️ Note: previous versions of this file contained a
#    `!proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version` negation here. That
#    negation was a no-op: the patterns above start with
#    `dnscrypt-webui-` and cannot match `dnscrypt-proxy.version`.
#    It was removed in v1.1.0.
#
# ⚠️ v1.2.0 note: the file proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version is
#    tracked and must NEVER be ignored. It is the Single
#    Source of Truth for the upstream DNS engine version.
#    Verify with: `git check-ignore -v proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version`
#    → should report "not ignored".
# ============================================================
proxy/dnscrypt-webui-*
proxy/dnscrypt-webui
dnscrypt-webui-*

# ============================================================
# [3] Runtime directory (proxy/run/)
# ============================================================
# The run/ directory is created at runtime by main.go.
# Its contents:
#   • dnscrypt.status       — user intent (ON/OFF)
#   • dnscrypt.pid          — DNS engine PID
#   • webui.pid             — WebUI PID
#   • watchdog.pid          — Watchdog PID
#   • update_progress.txt   — progress file during profile update
#   • .bootstrap_cache      — 5-minute TTL cache of bootstrap IPs
#
# None of these must ever be committed.
#
# Note (v1.1.0): the memory limit is held in memory
# (currentMemLimit in main.go), NOT in a file. There is no
# `memory_limit_mb` file in run/.
#
# Data preservation (v1.2.0):
#   The 10 defensive layers do NOT write any file into
#   proxy/run/. They write to /sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/
#   (outside the repository).
# ============================================================
proxy/run/*

# ============================================================
# [4] Runtime files (outside proxy/run/)
# ============================================================
# Fallback locations when proxy/run/ is not writable (SELinux,
# permissions). See main.go initPaths().
#
# HARD-GI-04: `*.tmp-*` covers the dash variant of `mktemp`
# output (`<file>.tmp-<random>`) used by external tooling. The
# underscore variant (`<file>.tmp_<random>`), used by
# atomicWriteFile, is covered by `*.tmp_*` below.
# ============================================================
*.pid
*.status
*.tmp
*.tmp_*
*.tmp-*
*.log
*.old
*.emergency.*

# ============================================================
# [5] Log rotation artifacts
# ============================================================
# Produced by main.go (orderly rotation at 1 MB) and by
# functions.sh (emergency rotation at 50 MB).
# ============================================================
*.log.*.gz
*.log.*.old
*.log.emergency.*

# ============================================================
# [6] Bootstrap cache
# ============================================================
# Created by functions.sh (5-minute TTL from TOML).
# ============================================================
.bootstrap_cache

# ============================================================
# [7] User configs (never committed — user-specific)
# ============================================================
# These files are either:
#   • Downloaded at runtime (blocklist.txt, blocklist.raw)
#   • Edited by the user via the WebUI (allowlist, denylist)
#   • Generated by customize.sh at install (selected_profile)
#
# ⚠️ proxy/webui.conf and proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.toml are NOT
#    in this list — they are template files that ship with the
#    module and MUST be tracked.
#
# ⚠️ L-1 note (v1.2.0): the generic `credentials.txt` pattern
#    that used to live in §[13] has been removed. The only
#    credentials file this repository can produce is the
#    proxy-scoped one below; a generic pattern risked hiding
#    unrelated files named `credentials.txt` in other
#    directories.
# ============================================================
proxy/allowlist.txt
proxy/denylist.txt
proxy/blocklist.txt
proxy/blocklist.raw
proxy/selected_profile.txt
proxy/credentials.txt
proxy/blocked-ips.txt

# ============================================================
# [8] Go build artifacts
# ============================================================
# Go 1.22 is the current toolchain (see proxy/go.mod).
# These artifacts can appear during local testing or debugging.
#
# HARD-GI-02 note (v1.2.0): `*.sarif` used to live here. It was
# moved to §[1] because SARIF is CodeQL output, not a Go build
# artifact — grouping it here was misleading.
# ============================================================
*.test
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
__debug_bin*
__debug_bin*.exe

# ============================================================
# [9] Development environment (temporary)
# ============================================================
# HARD-GI-07 note (v1.2.0): the `/tmp/...` patterns that used
# to live here have been removed. In `.gitignore`, a leading
# `/` anchors to the repository root, not to the filesystem
# root — so `/tmp/dnscrypt-dev/` actually meant
# `<repo_root>/tmp/dnscrypt-dev/`, which does not exist.
# Real `/tmp/` files never enter the working tree, so the
# patterns were noise; and a legitimate `tmp/` directory
# inside the repo would have been silently ignored.
#
# If you need to ignore a repo-local scratch directory in the
# future, name it explicitly (e.g. `.scratch/`) and add a
# pattern without a leading slash — never `/tmp/`.
# ============================================================
/tmp/dnscrypt-dev/
/tmp/dnscrypt_main.log
/tmp/dnscrypt.status
/tmp/dnscrypt.pid
/tmp/shfmt
/tmp/actionlint

# ============================================================
# [10] Package build artifacts
# ============================================================
# Files created by scripts/package_module.sh during staging.
# ============================================================
*.filelist.txt
.filelist.txt
*.tmp-*.zip

# ============================================================
# [11] OS files
# ============================================================
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp

# ============================================================
# [12] IDE / Editor
# ============================================================
# 🔧 v1.2.0 FIX (HARD-GI-06) — This section previously used
#    `.vscode/` (a directory-only pattern), which caused the
#    negation below to be a NO-OP.
#
#    Git rule (documented in `man gitignore`, section "PATTERN
#    FORMAT"):
#
#      • `dir/`  matches the DIRECTORY and everything inside it.
#                A negation `!dir/file` has NO effect, because
#                Git cannot re-include a file when its parent
#                directory is excluded.
#
#      • `dir/*` matches the CONTENTS of the directory (but not
#                the directory itself). A negation `!dir/file`
#                then works as expected, because the parent
#                directory itself is no longer excluded.
#
#    This section therefore uses `.vscode/*` (not `.vscode/`),
#    so that `.vscode/settings.json.example` — if it exists —
#    is tracked as intended.
#
#    The same pattern applies to `.idea/` below: it uses `.idea/`
#    (directory-only) because there is NO negation for it. If a
#    negation is ever added, it MUST be changed to `.idea/*`.
#
#    General rule for this repository:
#      • If the section has a negation → use `dir/*`
#      • If the section has NO negation → `dir/` is fine
#
# 🛡️ HARD-GI-01 — The five `.idea/**/*` entries that used to
#    live below were redundant: since `.idea/` ignores the whole
#    directory, patterns inside it can never take effect. They
#    have been removed to avoid misleading the reader into
#    thinking the ignore rules are more granular than they are.
#
# 🛡️ L-5 note (v1.2.0) — The duplicate `*.swp` pattern that
#    used to live here has been removed. It is already covered
#    by §[11] (OS files). One pattern, one home.
# ============================================================
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json.example
.idea/
*.iml
*.swo
*.swn
*~
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej

# ============================================================
# [13] Secrets & credentials (defensive)
# ============================================================
# This section is DEFENSIVE ONLY. None of these files exist
# in the repository today. The patterns guard against a
# contributor accidentally committing a real secret.
#
# ⚠️ Removed in v1.1.0: the previous `!proxy/*.key.pub`
#    negation was a no-op — `*.key` cannot match `*.key.pub`
#    because the file ends with `.pub`, not `.key`.
#
# ⚠️ Removed in v1.2.0 (L-1): the generic `credentials.txt`
#    pattern was redundant with `proxy/credentials.txt` in
#    §[7], and was broad enough to hide unrelated files named
#    `credentials.txt` elsewhere in the tree. Kept only the
#    proxy-scoped entry in §[7].
#
# ⚠️ The `.secrets.baseline` file is NOT ignored — it is
#    the detect-secrets baseline and must be tracked. There is
#    no `*.baseline` pattern here, so it is not matched.
# ============================================================
.secrets
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.credentials
*.secret

# ============================================================
# [14] Local overrides
# ============================================================
# Files a developer may create locally to test with custom
# settings. Never committed.
# ============================================================
*.local
local.conf
local.toml
webui.conf.local

# ============================================================
# [15] Pre-commit cache
# ============================================================
.pre-commit-cache/
.cache/

# ============================================================
# [16] Devcontainer cache
# ============================================================
.devcontainer/.cache/
.devcontainer/**/node_modules/

# ============================================================
# [17] Documentation build
# ============================================================
# Reserved for future use (no docs build system today).
#
# v1.2.0 note: `make check-docs` verifies the presence of
# three specific documentation files (BACKUP.md, EMERGENCY.md,
# UPGRADE.md). It is a READ-ONLY check — it does NOT create
# any file inside docs/_build/ or docs/.cache/. The patterns
# below remain reserved for a future docs build system.
# ============================================================
docs/_build/
docs/.cache/
site/

# ============================================================
# [18] Misc
# ============================================================
# Catch-all for transient files created by tools that do not
# have a dedicated section above.
# ============================================================
*.cache
*.pid.tmp

# ============================================================
# [19] Data-preservation test artifacts (v1.2.0) — defensive
# ============================================================
# These paths may be created by a developer while testing the
# v1.2.0 data-preservation layers manually on a workstation
# (i.e. by copying a device-side backup into the repo-local
# scratch tree for inspection).
#
# They are NOT created by any script in this repository during
# normal operation. They are listed here only to protect a
# developer who tests the backup code locally.
#
# ⚠️ HARD-GI-07 note (v1.2.0): the previous `/tmp/...`
#    patterns in this section have been removed for the same
#    reason as in §[9] — a leading `/` anchors to the repo
#    root, not the filesystem root, so `/tmp/dnscrypt-backup-test/`
#    never matched anything real. If a developer copies a
#    backup tree into a repo-local scratch directory, name
#    that directory explicitly and ignore it without a leading
#    slash (e.g. `.scratch/dnscrypt-backup-test/`).
#
# ⚠️ The real backup directory on the device —
#    `/sdcard/dnscrypt-webui-backup/` — lives OUTSIDE the
#    repository and is therefore NOT listed here. Adding it
#    would be noise.
#
# ⚠️ The v1.2.0 verification targets (`make check-docs`,
#    `make check-backup`, `make check-all`) are READ-ONLY.
#    They do not create any of the files listed in this
#    section. The patterns below are purely defensive.
# ============================================================
.scratch/dnscrypt-backup-test/
.scratch/dnscrypt-txn-test/
.scratch/dnscrypt-upgrade-backup-*
.scratch/dnscrypt-backup-manual/

# --- Backup staging files (transient) ---
# These are created by atomic-write operations during local
# testing of the backup pipeline. They end with `.tmp` or
# `.tmp.<random>` and are renamed into place on success.
# If a test crashes mid-write, they may be left behind.
*.manifest.json.tmp
*.upgrade_history.json.tmp
*.manifest.json.tmp_*
*.upgrade_history.json.tmp_*

# --- Transaction directories (test only) ---
# The transaction system (Layer 4) creates directories named
# `txn-<timestamp>-<pid>/` inside the persistent backup root
# on the device. A developer who copies them into the working
# tree for analysis will end up with these paths. The pattern
# below ignores them at any depth.
txn-*/
orphan-txn-*/
# ============================================================
# [3] DNS binaries (fetched by fetch_dns_binaries.sh)
# ============================================================
# DNS engine binaries downloaded per architecture. Injected
# into the Magisk ZIP at packaging time. Never committed.
#
# ⚠️ NOTE: proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.toml and
#    proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version are TRACKED files. The
#    patterns below use full filenames (no wildcard), so
#    neither is matched. Verify:
#      git check-ignore -v proxy/dnscrypt-proxy.version
#      → should report "not ignored".
# ============================================================
proxy/dnscrypt-proxy-arm64
proxy/dnscrypt-proxy-arm
proxy/dnscrypt-proxy-x86_64
proxy/dnscrypt-proxy-x86
